#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069940 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1$. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4$, and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n(2x)^{2} + 2(2x)(3) + (3)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} + 12x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1$, we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} + 2ab + b^{2} &= (a + b)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$4x^{2} + 12x + 9|=; (2x)^{2} + 12x + (3)^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(2x)^{2} + 2(6x) + (3)^{2}$\n3. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}$:\n$(2x)^{2} + 2(2x \\cdot 3) + (3)^{2}$\n4. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3$:\n$(2x + 3)^{2}$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d43b5e8-672e-5cdd-b7a3-8de29e6552f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1597 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069941 :tutorial/content "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n$-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,$?\nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25y^{2} - 20y + 4 &=  \\\\\n(5y)^{2} - 20y + 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n(5y)^{2} - 2(10y) + 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n(5y)^{2} - 2(5y \\cdot 2) + 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (5y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (25y^{2} - 20y + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= - (5y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "757d1827-0402-5d3d-b8da-a4dc88c254bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1598 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069954 :tutorial/content "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} - 2ab + b^{2} &= (a - b)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$9x^{2} - 24x + 16|=; (3x)^{2} - 24x + (4)^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3x)^{2} - 24x + (4)^{2}|=; (3x)^{2} - 2(12x) + (4)^{2}$\n3. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}$:\n$(3x)^{2} - 2(12x) + (4)^{2}|=; (3x)^{2} - 2(3x \\cdot 4) + (4)^{2}$\n4. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4$:\n$(3x)^{2} - 2(3x \\cdot 4) + (4)^{2}|=; (3x - 4)^{2}$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "609ce9b5-1b4e-5dfc-9d22-4ec6ea2d640b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1856 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"}]}
